00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:12.310 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  hi everybody  thank you for coming to my talk today my  name is scott churchill i'm the vice  president of scientific development at  mcr labs  we do compliance testing for cannabis  and cannabis products  we're going to be taking a look at an  evaluation we did on the impact of a  growing environment on the production of  secondary metabolites by cannabis plants  in addition to  being the vice president here i'm also  one of the founders and this was my  first company where  i was part of a startup  and a number of the  experiences i had i considered to be  stressors and those stressors as i was  going through that forced me to adapt by  developing new techniques uh greater  knowledge base  resources for dealing with a variety of  things  and that kind of  is what we're looking at today with  plants if we have um you know different  stressors do we get different responses  from the plants  so  um  when you when you think about cannabis  cultivation options you have  indoor and outdoor you you have  greenhouses and and all of those  different environments have different um  things associated with them in out in  indoor grow allows you to control  a lot more tightly all of the variables  um that the plant will  be facing  it also allows you to have a perpetual  harvest and you're likely to be able to  better control microbiological  contamination indoors and prevent any  kind of  accidental exposure to pesticides from a  nearby  farmer  putting down  pesticides on their crops  but it is more expensive and you it is a  heavier impact on the environment in  terms of  the lighting and the heating and the  cooling and the control of the  environment overall so  on the outdoor grows you get you know  free sunshine you get free rain and you  have um  less expense associated with  trying to scale agro  and  there's a lot of a lot of good reasons  but you need the right environment and  you don't have as tight a control over  some of the variables that we mentioned  a greenhouse is a is a good compromise  and it to the extent and to the way you  leverage it you can get some of the  benefits from both indoors or outdoor  growing  but  what is the impact on plant compound  production  between these different that's the  question you know we're going to be  taking a closer look at  but when we look at that we have to take  into account that there are a lot of  different  variables that affect  what a plant's going to produce  the first and most obvious one is the  plant genetics you have some plants that  have been  selectively bred for a period of time to  produce  either high thc numbers or high cbd  numbers or some kind of ratio of the two  people are looking at cultivars  specifically for cbg or other types of  cannabinoids they're selectively  breeding them to  turn off or on things and when you do  that you  as you continuously do that over time  you start to trim the branches of  possibilities of those plants and what  they can produce  other factors that  could affect the plants  production of secondary metabolites is  nutrition and irrigation  lighting whether it's  indoors with a limited spectrum and  tight control over time and intensity or  outdoors where the intensity changes and  the time changes depending on the season  and where you are  and the orientation  indoors you have a lot more control  about centering the light on the plants  whereas  outdoors you have to pick where you're  going to put your plants and let them  take advantage of the sun that way  so our our hypothesis was that we could  control for the genetics by  taking clones with the same genetic  information  and  use that control to see if they express  different secondary metabolites as a  function of their growing condition  we did this by  enlisting three cultivators who  had a total of five different cultivars  and for each cultivar we took a clone  and we split it between indoor and  outdoor growing environments  we  had the cultivators then take it through  vegetation flowering drying and curing  to a finished flower product  and then they brought the samples to two  laboratories  for analysis  in the two laboratories one of them was  mine we employed a targeted  quantitative analysis where  we do the compliance testing so we test  for all of the  usual compounds 17 cannabinoids 23  terpenes 4 heavy metals  and we do that using  lcuv gcf id and icpms  our partner lab  that collaborated with us on this was  psyx and they used a qtos they did an  untargeted search where anything that  ionized  they collected spectra on  and then would do an analysis of well  they did two things one is they did  principal component analysis to see if  there were  categories of compounds that were unique  to indoor versus outdoor as well as um  being able to take the some of the  spectra  that were obtained for the different  growing conditions  and compare it to a library to to get a  pretty good idea of of what we were  looking at  so um we're going to  take a look at the results we're going  to summarize the overall findings then  we're going to look a little bit at the  quantitative results followed by the  qualitative results the untargeted  search  the results show  what we were expecting to find which is  that  the  the trends show higher secondary  metabolite production in outdoor grown  plants  the  untargeted shows uh not only that the  compounds uh secondary metabolites were  elevated in the  in the outdoor grows but the type of  compound produced in the outdoor grows  was  different had a different chemical  profile  than the indoor growth  we also observed that there were strain  specific trends this is where  the genetics of the plant had a greater  impact on what they produced  versus the  environmental conditions whether it was  indoor or outdoor  did not have as as high an impact  we also  took two of the  cultivars a total of four plants indoor  and outdoor and we did heavy metals  analysis on it and we had a  interesting finding there where  in both cases the indoor samples showed  detection of cadmium where the outdoor  samples did not  it's a very limited data set and we're  going to have to take a closer look at  that  down the road  this  table shows that in 5 out of 5 samples  the terpenes and the cbga  in the targeted search were elevated in  outdoor  outdoor grown samples over indoor grown  samples  in 4 out of 5 cases delta 9 thc which is  a degradation product of thca  was elevated in the indoor samples and  in  three out of five cases thca was was  higher in the in the outdoor samples  we also  like i said before saw that there was  cadmium detected in the two indoor  samples as compared to outdoor samples  that we looked at  this is the um  principal component plot for the indoor  outdoor differences and this is just  showing you that the  principal component 2 is  consistently elevated in the indoor  samples and  depressed in the outdoor samples  this is a breakdown of the different  analytical targets that created those  principal components and we're going to  be taking a look a little bit later  at some of the outdoor the  targets that were identified to be  associated with outdoor  ground samples  and  this is where we got  those targets from  this shows the unique strains where  you know field trip number nine and  starfighter  both their chemical profiles whether  they were grown indoors or outdoors did  not dramatically change  um how how their  their secondary metabolite production  was geared towards so  they they are examples uh likely of uh  selective breeding kind of minimizing  um how much variation you can expect by  varying the the environment that you're  you're growing your plants and the  inputs  and this is an example of one of the  outdoor  target outdoor associated targets that  we identified  it's got a mass to charge ratio of 271  and  on the left hand side you can see it's  always elevated in outdoor and never  elevated on the indoor and on the  right-hand side that's just a plot that  allows you to see the difference between  the outdoor a little bit easier  so we did a library match to this and it  it  matched up very well to apogenin  this is a compound that's of therapeutic  interest because of its  antioxidant anti-inflammatory  and blood pressure reducing  characteristics it's also antibacterial  and antiviral so it's being looked at  for a number of  different applications it might be  important  to  to have plants that express this if  that's  something that you're trying to  accomplish  and it's heavily  it's heavily biased towards producing  more of it in the outdoor samples versus  the indoor samples  now  on on this one we see the same trend  it's  an unknown with a mass charge ratio of  433  and it's  always elevated in outdoor it's it's  hardly detected in the indoor samples  and  we did the library match and it turns  out that it's by texan  vitexin has also got the therapeutic  potential it's  thought to it's they're hopefully  looking at it for potentially  modulating hormones reducing the  severity and the symptoms of ems  and they're also looking at  treating migraines and joint conditions  with it  the trend continues with this  outdoor always elevated indoor not  really present and it turns out that  it's a sugar conjugated version of  vitaxin  by texan ramnicide and it's also being  evaluated as a potential therapeutic  i didn't see anything specific for that  alone i saw it mentioned in studies that  were looking at flavonoids in general  which is a  hot topic in  plant therapeutics  and then we also saw  an unknown at 449 master charge ratio  again elevated and outdoors not present  so much in the indoor  and it was a library match for orienten  that's  being looked at for anti-aging and  anti-cancer treatments and i didn't  mention it on the previous slides but if  you look at these compounds they are  very oxygenated as compared to the  typical terpene profiles  the outdoor grown samples tend to have  more oxygenation they tend to have  more sugar conjugation they tend to be  larger more complicated molecules as a  as a differentiation between the indoors  and the outdoors so  um and then  you know in  in some instances that i highlighted  here there are compounds of therapeutic  interest  so  going back to the the variables that we  talked about um you know indoor versus  outdoor  uh and and the fact that we we did see  um you know two strains that had heavy  metals detected in the indoor  we don't have enough information to know  whether or not uh feeding or water  source was was the cause of that so  that's something that we're going to  try and look at a little bit more  carefully in a separate study  but we did we were able to  see an increase in the amount and type  of secondary metabolites produced in  outdoor samples versus indoor samples  overall  and in the outdoor samples we were able  to identify  principal components to separate indoor  versus outdoor  the indoor samples were biased towards  producing the more common  routinely measured  lighter  simpler  terpene  secondary metabolites whereas the  outdoors  had the  the larger or more  complex molecules being produced  in the outdoor samples so  we also saw that some strains do not  respond to the different environments  that they're in they they basically  produce what they produce and  you can do it indoors and outdoors and  get very similar results so  very very interesting findings um  i think that  it you know in conclusion  um  the cultivation method does impact both  the uh the type and amount of secondary  metabolites that are produced  this this phenomena can be  somewhat diminished by  specific strains  my current theory is that it has to do  with how much selective breeding has  gone on and how much the  the options of  the plants have been trimmed out by  those breeding programs  and  of interest for further studies is  trying to identify  which environmental conditions from the  outdoors are  reproducible indoors that way you would  be able to  put in the appropriate  conditions to get indoor grows to  produce um the  the  secondary metabolites that might be of  therapeutic interest that that could be  important in in places where you don't  have outdoor growing as an option  and it will be important if you want to  have perpetual harvest  if you want to have tighter control over  microbial contamination  but nevertheless the  the fact that the  production of metabolites is impacted  this means that when you're  when you're making decisions about your  grow you have to factor in in addition  to all of the factors that we talked in  in the beginning  in terms of the cost and the the control  that you get and the freedom and so  forth you also have to ask whether or  not you're attempting to produce these  molecules of interest  and until we can find a way to tease out  the the outdoor conditions that result  in those molecules outdoor is is the  only way that that i'm aware of that you  can  kind of put the plant in a position to  make those molecules um or favor those  molecules at least  um so for future studies that's what  we're going to be looking at as well as  taking out  a separate study to look at heavy metals  and whether or not there are  conditions  that are in the outdoors versus the  indoors that are impacting whether or  not they take up heavy metals  and um seeing if we can identify those  as a possible way to prevent you know  those those types of accumulations  and with that i want to  thank everybody for your time and i want  to let you know that if there are  any questions you can reach out to me  i'm at scott c mcrlabs.com  and i'd like to hear anything that you  guys have to say about this or any  questions that you might have  always looking forward  looking for opportunities to collaborate  with the community and answer questions  that otherwise can't be answered without  access to analytics so  in addition to the compliance testing we  do we try to add in  experiments and initiatives and  collaborations that allow us to  answer questions that are important to  the community  with that thank you and have a great day  you

